What was run, and how (2026-10-31, Linux x86_64, rustc 1.95.0 = the repo's rust-toolchain.toml)
Source: openai/codex @ 67f0994b532b8740f14b5b738af7b7b2a915b1d6 (the commit the article reads; not updated).

Why not `cargo build -p ...` in the repo: cargo resolves the whole 166-member workspace first and fetches its git
dependencies (rusty_v8 with its Chromium submodules was 3.1 GB before I killed it). So I built a scratch workspace
(harness/workspace.Cargo.toml, the repo's Cargo.lock copied for version pins) holding UNMODIFIED copies of:
  codex-rs/execpolicy                  -> package codex-execpolicy
  codex-rs/apply-patch                 -> package codex-apply-patch   (plus harness/verify_probe.rs, a 15-line extra bin)
  codex-rs/utils/{absolute-path,path-uri}
  codex-rs/shell-command/src/{bash.rs, shell_detect.rs, powershell.rs, command_safety/}  -> package shellprobe
Two stand-ins, both mine and labelled in the files:
  harness/exec_server_stub.rs   codex-exec-server stub: the five ExecutorFileSystem methods apply-patch calls, over tokio::fs
                                (the real one is the 100+ crate exec-server; it also handles sandboxing and symlinks)
  powershell_parser.rs line 128 commented out in the shellprobe copy (a Windows-only test line needing codex-protocol)

Commands (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, nice 15, under the heavy lock):
  cargo build -p codex-execpolicy ; cargo test -p codex-execpolicy      -> 7 + 29 tests passed, 0 failed
  cargo build -p shellprobe ; cargo test -p shellprobe                  -> 82 passed, 0 failed (real bash.rs / is_dangerous_command tests)
  cargo build -p codex-apply-patch ; cargo test -p codex-apply-patch --lib -> 65 passed, 3 failed (see below)
  python3 -I verify/run_execpolicy.py ... ; python3 -I verify/run_apply_patch.py ...   (logs: *_run.log, results: *_real.json)

The 3 apply-patch unit-test failures are not findings about Codex:
  test_apply_patch_fails_on_write_error, test_failed_move_returns_committed_destination_delta
      set a directory to mode 0555 and expect the write to fail; this box runs as root, so it does not.
  test_delete_symlink_returns_inexact_delta
      needs the real filesystem layer's follow_symlinks=false handling; my stub ignores the flag.

Not run: codex-core (exec_policy.rs, the orchestrator, compaction), codex-linux-sandbox, anything needing the whole workspace.
A direct `codex-execpolicy check ... rm -rf x` on the command line was refused by the permission layer's rm check
(the arguments are only data to the checker); the same 32 commands were passed from a script file instead.
Build outputs (about 2.3 GB of target/) were deleted afterwards.
